Workflow Metadata
name: Portfolio  
This is the name of your workflow. It will show up in the GitHub Actions tab as “Portfolio”.

Triggers
on:  
Defines when this workflow should run.

push:  
Runs the workflow whenever code is pushed.

branches: → - main  
Restricts it to only run when changes are pushed to the main branch.

workflow_dispatch: null  
Allows you to manually trigger the workflow from the GitHub Actions UI.

Permissions
permissions:  
Defines what this workflow can access.

pages: write → Grants permission to publish to GitHub Pages.

id-token: write → Required for authentication when deploying securely.

Jobs
jobs:  
A workflow is made up of jobs. Here you have one job called deploy.

Deploy Job
deploy:  
The job name.

runs-on: ubuntu-latest  
Specifies the runner environment (Ubuntu VM).

environment:  
Declares the environment for deployment.

name: github-pages → Marks this job as deploying to GitHub Pages.

url: ${{ steps.deployment.outputs.page_url }} → Dynamically sets the environment URL to the deployed site’s link (provided by the deployment step).

Steps
Steps are executed sequentially inside the job:

- name: Checkout code

uses: actions/checkout@v4  
This pulls your repository’s code into the runner so subsequent steps can access it.

- name: Setup GitHub Pages

uses: actions/configure-pages@v4  
Configures the Pages environment (sets up authentication, permissions, etc.).

- name: upload static files

uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v4

with: path: .  
Uploads your site’s files (here, everything in the current directory .) as an artifact that will be deployed.

- name: deploy to GitHub Pages

id: deployment  
Gives this step an identifier so its outputs (like page_url) can be referenced later.

uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4  
Actually deploys the uploaded artifact to GitHub Pages. This step produces the page_url output.

Why the URL Might Not Show
The url field depends on steps.deployment.outputs.page_url. If:

The id: deployment is missing, or

The action doesn’t produce page_url,

then the environment won’t display the link. In your case, since you’re using actions/deploy-pages@v4 with id: deployment, it should work — but the URL only appears once the deployment succeeds.

✅ In short:
This workflow builds and uploads your static site, then deploys it to GitHub Pages, and finally shows the live site URL in the GitHub Actions summary.
